## The 14-day spa cure in Mariánské Lázně — the optimal programme

Two weeks. That is the length of stay most often chosen by self-paying guests, and for a number of indications it is also the length the [Czech indication list](/en/indications-and-contraindications) assigns to a repeat stay in contributory care (P 14). Basic stays covered by insurance tend to be longer — 21 or 28 days in comprehensive care, 14 or 21 days in contributory care. The specialist literature regards two to three weeks with 10–21 treatments as the usual length of spa treatment; a stay shorter than ten days with fewer than ten treatments no longer counts as balneotherapy ([Maraver et al., 2020](https://doi.org/10.1007/s00484-020-02041-5); [Karagülle and Karagülle, 2021](https://consensus.app/papers/details/14ac08e16e2d5bbb804b6330ab17303b/), *Int J Biometeorol*). A two-week cure is therefore the shortest option that still meets what spa medicine considers treatment.

In Mariánské Lázně (Marienbad), longer stays have a long tradition. Goethe spent whole weeks here, as did the English king Edward VII. What awaits you over fourteen days is more than wellness: a systematic programme under the supervision of a spa physician that draws on the local healing resources — more than [40 mineral springs](/en/mineral-springs) of differing chemical composition, natural CO₂ from the Maria Spring, peat from the Slavkov Forest and the forest climate at 630 metres above sea level.

The schedule in this article is an example. The actual plan is drawn up by the spa physician at the initial examination according to your diagnosis, and adjusted during the stay.

## Before you arrive

### Preparation

Bring your current medical reports — blood count, X-rays for orthopaedic complaints, cardiology findings for circulatory problems. The spa physician uses them to draw up a more precise treatment plan.

Pack for two weeks: comfortable daywear, swimwear, sturdy shoes for forest paths, smart clothing for dinner. A detailed list is at the end of this article.

## Week 1: Adaptation and diagnostics

### Days 1–2: Arrival

The first day is for settling in. Unpack, explore the hotel, walk to the Colonnade. Take your first sip from the Cross Spring — slowly, while walking, as tradition dictates.

Day two: the initial examination with the spa physician. Blood pressure, pulse, mobility, medical history — everything is recorded. On this basis the physician draws up an individual treatment plan for the next thirteen days. They determine which springs you should drink from, which treatments you will have, in what order and at what intensity, and which treatments are not suitable for you.

### Days 3–4: A gentle start

Treatments begin cautiously:

**Drinking cure (daily):** A morning walk to the Colonnade, 200–300 ml of healing water from the prescribed spring, in sips over 20 minutes. Every day from now on.

**Mineral bath:** The first bath in mineral water at 34–36 °C. The carbon dioxide in the water creates a fine fizzing on the skin and dilates the cutaneous vessels. 20 minutes in the water, then 30 minutes of rest.

**Relaxation massage:** A gentle full-body massage to open the spa week. 30 minutes.

**Afternoon:** Light walks through the spa park and to the Colonnade. The body is adjusting to the new rhythm.

### Days 5–7: Stepping up

From the fifth day, more specific treatments are added:

[**Dry CO₂ gas bath**](/en/magazine/co2-gas-injections)**:** You lie in a gas bath flooded with natural carbon dioxide. The gas penetrates the skin and dilates the cutaneous vessels; in a study of 46 patients, ten dry CO₂ baths measurably raised the surface temperature, and thus the blood flow, of a number of body regions ([Zbroja et al., 2021](https://consensus.app/papers/details/55f7781cf9f7502ea76768a853ffc7da/), *IJERPH*). Spa physicians prescribe it above all for illnesses of the circulatory system, which are among the indications of Mariánské Lázně. Usually 3× a week.

[**Peat wrap**](/en/mineral-springs)**:** Peat from the Slavkov Forest, heated to 42 °C, applied to the back, joints or abdomen. The slowly released heat warms the tissues through and eases muscle tension. Usually 2–3× a week.

**Inhalation:** Finely atomised mineral water for the airways. The physician prescribes it for chronic diseases of the airways; a systematic review of 27 studies describes better nasal patency and mucociliary clearance after inhalations of thermal waters, with the caveat that the studies are of low quality ([Fontana et al., 2025](https://consensus.app/papers/details/e377f688a5f85f3c9e6c9373d675eb8e/), *Int J Biometeorol*).

**Note:** Mild tiredness in the first days is common. The body is adapting to many new stimuli. Allow yourself an afternoon rest, and tell the physician about any more pronounced fatigue or worsening of your complaints.

## Week 2: The full programme

### Days 8–10: Full intensity

The programme now runs at full scale. The body has grown used to the rhythm of treatments and rest.

**A typical daily schedule:**

- 7:00 — Drinking cure at the Colonnade
- 8:00 — Breakfast
- 9:00 — Dry CO₂ gas bath (25 min) + rest
- 10:30 — Peat wrap (20 min) + rest
- 12:00 — Lunch
- 13:00–15:00 — Rest
- 15:00 — Massage or electrotherapy
- 16:30 — Terrain therapy (prescribed forest walk)
- 19:00 — Dinner

**Gas injections:** In the second week the physician may add subcutaneous CO₂ injections — a small amount of natural gas applied with a fine needle under the skin around the joints or spine. It is a traditional Marienbad treatment that the spa physician adds as a complement to wraps, baths and physiotherapy. The application takes a few minutes; the injection site may be briefly tender.

**Electrotherapy:** Treatment with a weak current, used by physiotherapists as a complement for pain and muscle tension.

### Days 11–12: Special treatments

**Underwater massage:** A combination of warm mineral water and targeted water pressure. Used for joint stiffness and muscle spasms.

**The Roman Baths at Ensana Nové Lázně:** An architectural jewel — original mosaics from 1896, marble columns, a warm and a cold pool. An experience that unites history with spa tradition.

**Paraffin wrap:** Warming of the hands and feet for joint complaints.

### Days 13–14: Conclusion

**Final examination:** The spa physician compares your current values with the findings at admission — blood pressure, mobility, subjective complaints — and assesses how you tolerated the treatment.

**Recommendations for home:** The physician issues a written report with recommendations — exercises, continuing the drinking cure with bottled mineral water, and possibly the timing of your next stay.

**Last treatments:** A final mineral bath and massage as a gentle coda.

